WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.130
Sound-launch questions become much easier as soon as the learner asks what is vibrating before asking what is travelling.

2
00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:12.260
The speaker cone, beacon, or tuning fork is the source, and its vibration rate sets the cycle rate that enters the air.

3
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:18.390
The air matters too, but in a different role.

4
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:24.520
It relays the disturbance as compressions and rarefactions instead of choosing a new frequency for itself.

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.650
That is why the safest opening move for this lesson is source first, medium second, meaning third.

6
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:36.780
Keep this takeaway in view: This lesson settles down once the source is treated as the owner of the vibration rate and the medium is treated as the relay.

7
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.910
Source means something must vibrate.

8
00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:49.040
A sound wave starts when a source repeatedly disturbs the nearby medium.

9
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.170
Frequency means launched cycle rate.

10
00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:01.300
The source vibration rate becomes the frequency of the launched sound.

11
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.880
This diagram matters because it shows two linked roles without mixing them.

12
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.460
The source vibrates and launches a repeating disturbance.

13
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:18.040
The nearby air is disturbed and passes that pattern onward, but the air does not choose a new cycle rate.

14
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.620
Reading the picture in two passes helps.

15
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:29.200
First read the source vibration.

16
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.780
Then read the medium relay.

17
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.360
Once those roles stay separate, the frequency calculation later feels honest instead of magical.

18
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.940
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong M9 answer reads the source action first and the medium relay second.

19
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:51.520
Launch point: The source begins each cycle by pushing and pulling on the nearby air.

20
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.100
Relay medium: The air passes the compression-rarefaction pattern from one region to the next.

21
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:02.867
A stadium-clap analogy helps because it separates beat-setting from beat-relaying.

22
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:08.634
One leader starts clapping at a definite rate.

23
00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:14.401
The crowd responds and carries the pattern around the stadium, but the crowd did not choose the original beat.

24
00:02:14.401 --> 00:02:20.168
In the same way, a vibrating source sets the cycle rate for the sound wave, while the medium transmits that pattern onward.

25
00:02:20.168 --> 00:02:25.935
The analogy is not perfect, but it is excellent for protecting the source-first habit before the lesson turns quantitative.

26
00:02:25.935 --> 00:02:31.702
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy makes the source-medium split feel natural before formulas appear.

27
00:02:31.702 --> 00:02:37.469
Beat setter means source role.

28
00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:43.236
One leader sets the rhythm just as the vibrating source sets the sound frequency.

29
00:02:43.236 --> 00:02:49.003
Relay crowd means medium role.

30
00:02:49.003 --> 00:02:54.770
The crowd carries the pattern onward just as the air relays the pressure disturbance.

31
00:02:54.770 --> 00:03:00.300
The frequency calculation is not a separate trick from the physics story.

32
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.830
Frequency equals number of vibrations divided by time, so it starts with the source count.

33
00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:11.360
Period is the time for one complete cycle, so period equals one over frequency.

34
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.890
The key sentence after the arithmetic is that the launched sound has the same frequency as the source because each source cycle launches one sound cycle into the air.

35
00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:22.420
That is the sentence the learner should still be able to say after the numbers are finished.

36
00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:27.950
Keep this takeaway in view: The calculation stays safe when the source count and the sound meaning are stated together.

37
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:33.480
Numerical step: Use the source vibration count and the elapsed time to find the frequency.

38
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.010
Meaning step: State that the sound in the air keeps the same cycle rate as the vibrating source.

39
00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:44.540
f = number of vibrations / time.

40
00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:50.070
T = 1 / f.

41
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:55.600
Count the source motion first.

42
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.130
Then explain what that count means for the sound in the medium.

43
00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:06.595
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

44
00:04:06.595 --> 00:04:12.061
First identify the vibrating source.

45
00:04:12.061 --> 00:04:17.526
Second calculate or read the source cycle rate using the information given.

46
00:04:17.526 --> 00:04:22.992
Third state what that cycle rate means for the launched sound in the medium.

47
00:04:22.992 --> 00:04:28.457
That three-part sequence keeps the explanation physical, mathematical, and communicative all at once.

48
00:04:28.457 --> 00:04:33.923
It also protects the learner from the weak habit of jumping straight to a number without saying what the number belongs to.

49
00:04:33.923 --> 00:04:39.388
Keep this takeaway in view: Source, cycle rate, and sound meaning is the safest opening routine for Lesson 1.

50
00:04:39.388 --> 00:04:44.854
Step 1 means find the source.

51
00:04:44.854 --> 00:04:50.319
Ask which object is actually vibrating and launching the disturbance.

52
00:04:50.319 --> 00:04:55.785
Step 2 means read the cycle rate.

53
00:04:55.785 --> 00:05:01.250
Use the vibration count or timing information to get the source frequency and period.

54
00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:06.010
Run four checks.

55
00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:10.770
Sound starts with a vibrating source.

56
00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:15.530
The source vibration rate sets the sound frequency.

57
00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:20.290
Period is the time for one complete cycle and equals one over frequency.

58
00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:25.050
And if the source stops vibrating, no new sound wave is launched even though the air is still present.

59
00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:29.810
Those four checks capture the whole lesson structure before the worked example begins.

60
00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:34.570
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain why the source owns the cycle rate.

61
00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:39.330
Origin means vibrating source.

62
00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:44.090
No new sound is launched unless something is vibrating.

63
00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:48.850
Frequency means source-owned.

64
00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:53.610
The source cycle rate becomes the sound frequency in the medium.

65
00:05:53.610 --> 00:06:00.006
Start by using the source motion to find the frequency.

66
00:06:00.006 --> 00:06:06.403
Then calculate the period if the question asks for it.

67
00:06:06.403 --> 00:06:12.799
After the numbers are done, say explicitly what they mean: the launched sound in the air has the same frequency as the vibrating source because each source cycle launches one sound cycle.

68
00:06:12.799 --> 00:06:19.195
If a follow-up question asks why no new sound appears when the source stops, return to the same routine and remove the vibration step.

69
00:06:19.195 --> 00:06:25.592
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

70
00:06:25.592 --> 00:06:31.988
Keep this takeaway in view: The Lesson 1 worked example improves immediately once the source role is named before the medium role.

71
00:06:31.988 --> 00:06:38.385
Do not skip the source: The frequency calculation only makes sense after the vibrating object is identified.

72
00:06:38.385 --> 00:06:44.781
Do not skip the meaning: Explain what the calculated frequency tells you about the sound wave launched into the air.

73
00:06:44.781 --> 00:06:51.177
identify the vibrating source.

74
00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:57.574
find f from the source count and T from 1 / f if needed.

75
00:06:57.574 --> 00:07:03.970
The best answers finish with meaning, not with a bare number.
